The coordinator of a neighborhood event publishes the meeting agenda
through a Unix service. The provider has authorized your individual
account to use a private submissions directory, not to change the
published files.
You have two documents called \texttt{agenda.txt}: a draft on your
laptop and a copy already on the server. Which copy will move, where
will it land, and how will you know it got there intact? These are
questions to answer before you press Enter.

\begin{objectives}
\item Read file transfer commands as source followed by destination.
\item Use SFTP's two directory contexts and recognize how they differ from SSH.
\item Preview a transfer locally and verify a file before treating it as delivered.
\end{objectives}

\section{Give each path a machine}\label{sec:39-endpoints}
\index{scp@\texttt{scp}}\index{file transfer}
Chapter~\ref{ch:38} used SSH to open a remote shell. A file transfer
over SSH uses the same host-identity and account checks, but it need
not give you a remote shell to type in. Start from a known local
terminal, confirm the account and host, and decide on a destination
filename that doesn't collide with anyone else's. The owner might ask
for a date or your team initials. That isn't bureaucracy for its own
sake: naming a file \texttt{agenda-new.txt} explains little after next
week's second revision.

These commands show forms for a service you are explicitly allowed to use.
The example host \texttt{files.example.org} is not a practice target.
Do not run these against the printed name. For illustrations the
provider has told you that \texttt{/home/learner/incoming/} exists, is
private to the account for this task, and permits the transfer. The
source file has been checked on your laptop and the destination filename
is reserved and confirmed unused. Use the actual paths supplied for
your account rather than assuming that directory layout.

Work out who will read the copy before you decide where to put it.
Your home directory may be good enough for a private rehearsal and
entirely wrong for an official submission. The best destination is
the one the provider intends, with the access the task requires.
Giving the coordinator the right to open a single submitted file
doesn't imply giving everyone the right to see everything else in
that directory. If a transfer fails for lack of permission, check
the path and ask the operator for the approved alternative. Don't
make your working directory broadly readable in an attempt to fix
an unrelated permission error. Copying a document doesn't change
its sensitivity, either. A confidential agenda is still confidential
after you put it in a file named \texttt{draft.txt}.

\begin{terminal}[title={Illustrative upload; not a live exercise}]
scp ./agenda.txt \
  learner@files.example.org:/home/learner/incoming/agenda-r1.txt
\end{terminal}

The first operand is the source. A leading \texttt{./} names a file in
the current local directory. In the second operand an account and host
are followed by a colon; everything after the colon is the destination
path on the server. The continuation backslash is a shell convenience,
not part of either filename. The utility does not erase the source.
If the target is an existing ordinary file, though, the old contents
can be overwritten. \cmd|scp| doesn't provide a dependable
``discard that transfer'' function. For a shared directory, asking a
colleague not to use a name is no guarantee of exclusivity: use personal
staging space or ask the operator for the documented submission workflow.
In Figure~\ref{fig:39-direction}, the arrow points from the first
operand toward the second no matter which way the bytes move.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-39/direction.tikz}
\caption{The first path supplies bytes; the second path receives them.}\label{fig:39-direction}
\VisualDescription{An upload starts at a local path and ends at an account, host, and remote path; a download reverses the direction.}
\end{figure}

For a download, put the remote source first. The destination below is a
new name in your local working directory, not a command to overwrite
your original draft. In this and the preceding example, check that the
present paths really refer to files; if you pass a directory for a
destination, copy tools may choose a filename inside it instead.

\begin{terminal}[title={Illustrative download into a confirmed new local path}]
scp learner@files.example.org:/home/learner/incoming/agenda-r1.txt \
  ./agenda-received.txt
\end{terminal}

\begin{quicktable}{p{1.4in}Y}
\toprule
Form or option & Decision it makes \\
\midrule
Local path first & Upload from the current machine if the destination is remote. \\
Remote path first & Download to the current machine if the destination is local. \\
\texttt{user@host:/path} & In \texttt{scp}, attach the path to a remote account and host. \\
\texttt{./draft:copy.txt} & In \texttt{scp}, a local name with a colon can be made unambiguous with an explicit local path. \\
\texttt{-P 2222} & In OpenSSH \texttt{scp} or \texttt{sftp}, use the authorized service's supplied port. SSH itself uses lowercase \texttt{-p}. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

The OpenSSH tools described here are not part of POSIX. Since OpenSSH
9.0, \cmd|scp| uses SFTP by default. An older client can behave
differently, notably in how remote path patterns are interpreted; the
local manual and provider's guidance matter. Avoid legacy protocol
switches, wildcards, home-directory abbreviations, and difficult
filenames when learning. A simple absolute remote path keeps attention on
the direction and destination. Shell quoting protects spaces from the
local shell, but older remote interpretation may add another parsing
step. When names are complicated, consider an interactive SFTP session
and check its own quoting instructions.

\section{Use SFTP when you want to see both desks}
\index{sftp@\texttt{sftp}}\index{SFTP}
The SFTP sequence is an alternative to the preceding \texttt{scp}
sequence, not a second upload to run after it. If you already submitted
the file, don't use the same destination name again. With \texttt{scp},
the remote path lives in the operand after \texttt{user@host:}; at
the SFTP prompt you're connected already, so the transfer subcommands
use pathnames without a host prefix. A relative name resolves against
the appropriate local or remote working directory. The two access methods
may expose different directory views; use the paths your provider
supplies for each rather than assuming the spelling carries over.

SFTP is a file-transfer protocol carried over SSH in this workflow.
It is not unencrypted FTP, nor the same as FTP protected by TLS.
When you start the OpenSSH \cmd|sftp| program and authenticate to your
verified service, its prompt accepts file transfer commands. This is
not a regular shell: commands at the SFTP prompt come from SFTP's
own vocabulary. Check its built-in \cmd|help| for the commands available
with your installed client. The following start command is illustrative.

\begin{terminal}[title={Illustrative SFTP start; not a live exercise}]
sftp learner@files.example.org
\end{terminal}

Some providers give an SFTP-only account access to a small view of
their storage. The remote working directory may be \texttt{/} as
seen by your SFTP client without being the machine's actual root
directory. A path that a support person sees as
\texttt{/home/learner/incoming} may appear to you as
\texttt{/incoming}. Get paths from the instructions for the access
method you're using. If SSH opens no interactive shell but SFTP
connects, the restriction may be intentional. A transfer failing
after a verified login can also mean you can read from a location
but cannot write to it. Look at which operation was denied rather
than assuming your password expired.

Inside SFTP, \cmd|pwd| prints the remote working directory. \cmd|lpwd|
prints the local one: the \texttt{l} is a mnemonic for local. Similarly
\cmd|ls| lists remote names and \cmd|lls| lists local names. \cmd|cd|
changes the remote working directory, \cmd|lcd| changes the local one.
Use these checks immediately before a transfer, particularly after
changing directory. The dollar-sign prompt in earlier parts of this
book belonged to the shell; \texttt{sftp>} in the following illustrated
session is a prompt too, not input.

\begin{session}
sftp> pwd
Remote working directory: /home/learner
sftp> lpwd
Local working directory: /home/guest/practice
sftp> cd /home/learner/incoming
sftp> ls
sftp> lls
agenda.txt
\end{session}

Figure~\ref{fig:39-sftp} shows two working directories connected by a
single SFTP prompt. The listed files do not necessarily tell you who
else can change either directory. An empty listing means the command
did not show any entries; it does not grant permission to place files
there or promise that a later command won't collide.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-39/sftp.tikz}
\caption{Inside SFTP, the two working directories are independent.}\label{fig:39-sftp}
\VisualDescription{One prompt operates on a local directory via lpwd and lls, and on a separate remote directory via pwd and ls.}
\end{figure}

\cmd|put| takes a local source and optional remote destination.
\cmd|get| takes a remote source and optional local destination.
The two names here differ on purpose, so you can inspect the received
data without changing the original. \cmd|bye| ends the SFTP session.

\begin{terminal}[title={Inside SFTP only, after checking both directories}]
put agenda.txt agenda-r1.txt
get agenda-r1.txt agenda-received.txt
bye
\end{terminal}

A transfer meter measures progress, not correctness. A failed copy
can leave a partial file, depending on the tool, server, and mode. If
the connection breaks, don't blindly rerun into the same path or send
a partial file on to someone else. Tell anyone waiting that it hasn't
been verified. Determine which path was being written, inspect it,
and if the service allows, transfer to a different unused name, then
verify and hand off that copy. For a service that consumes files
automatically, ask where to stage a complete file before submitting
it. Don't assume renaming can be undone or that it is safe across
different storage locations.

Names can be misleading in less dramatic ways. A coworker may
send \texttt{agenda.txt} as regular text and someone else may save
the new version as \texttt{Agenda.txt}. One filesystem might
consider those distinct names while another might treat them as
the same path. Names with spaces can be perfectly ordinary, but
it's worth rehearsing quoting separately from a transfer that
could replace a file. Use plain, unambiguous names for the first
copy, check the listing, and choose a name that still means
something to the person who has to find it later. If your exchange
requires accurate timestamps, permissions, links, or other
attributes, get the policy and test those attributes explicitly.
A transfer of a readable document is not a complete migration of
an account or filesystem. The lab verifies only bytes and paths.

\section{Preview and verify on one computer}\label{sec:39-practice}
\index{rsync@\texttt{rsync}}\index{cmp@\texttt{cmp}}
You can exercise the same habits with two directories. This lab
makes no remote connections. It uses POSIX shell syntax; \texttt{mktemp}
is common but not specified by POSIX. Use the trusted system temporary
directory or another trusted absolute path. Stop unless
\cmd|pwd| shows the newly created \texttt{utp-transfer} directory.
The directories stand for your desk and a staging area.

\begin{terminal}
transferlab=$(mktemp -d /tmp/utp-transfer.XXXXXXXX) &&
  cd "$transferlab" &&
  mkdir source receive &&
  pwd
\end{terminal}

In the confirmed lab, \texttt{>} writes \texttt{agenda.txt} in
the just-created source directory. Use this command only for the
synthetic lab.

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' '18:00 open the hall' '18:15 check the chairs' \
  > source/agenda.txt
\end{terminal}

If optional \cmd|rsync| is installed, preview a local transfer.
\texttt{-r} means descend into subdirectories; \texttt{-n} is a dry run
that doesn't copy bytes; \texttt{-i} itemizes planned changes.
Here they are written separately to keep the dry-run option visible.
A trailing slash on \texttt{./source/} means transfer the directory's
contents, not introduce a directory named \texttt{source} under
\texttt{receive}. Both paths are local because neither is a remote
specification. Consult your installed manual; Linux distributions,
macOS and the BSDs may package different versions or implementations,
and this exercise uses the upstream rsync interpretation.

\begin{terminal}
rsync -r -n -i ./source/ ./receive/
ls ./receive
\end{terminal}

You may see a line such as \texttt{>f+++++++++ agenda.txt}: in this
case the initial symbol indicates transfer, \texttt{f} means an
ordinary file, and the plus signs mark data or attributes for a new file.
The subsequent directory listing has no entries. A dry run predicts
what would happen given the observed state and chosen options.
It doesn't reserve disk space, freeze the source, or promise that the
actual write will succeed. Don't add deletion options to make a copy
look tidy; a receiving directory often contains information absent from
the source, and a mirror is not an archive of older versions.

If the itemized list is puzzling, change one operand at a time.
Ask what directory already exists at the destination, then ask
whether the source is the directory or what sits inside it. Compare
the result with Figure~\ref{fig:39-slash}. A preview can show a
directory entry and a file entry, since this example permits
recursion. In a real transfer, a preview that's unexpectedly empty
can mean the files look up to date to the tool, not that the source
was empty. Transfer utilities can compare timestamps and sizes
to decide which files to send. Don't interpret a quiet preview as
a byte comparison, and don't try to force an entire tree to copy
just because the preview doesn't print the filename you expected.
Use a small hand-picked test and verify the actual receiving bytes.
If you are previewing a real remote plan later, remember a dry run
can still connect, authenticate, and inspect remote filenames;
``dry'' refers to writes, not permission to examine another machine.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-39/contents.tikz}
\caption{In rsync, the source slash controls the receiving layout.}\label{fig:39-slash}
\VisualDescription{Contrasting examples place agenda.txt either inside receive/source or directly inside receive, depending on the source's trailing slash.}
\end{figure}

For the actual lab, copy just the one file to an unused name. Here
\texttt{-i} on \cmd|cp| (interactive, ask before overwrite) is not
rsync's itemize flag; options belong to individual commands.
If prompted, decline and inspect the destination before proceeding.
Neither the preview nor \cmd|cp| depends on the original agenda being
privileged or confidential. If \cmd|rsync| wasn't installed, you
can start with the explicit source and destination below and still do
the content check.

\begin{terminal}
cp -i ./source/agenda.txt ./receive/agenda-received.txt
cmp ./source/agenda.txt ./receive/agenda-received.txt
printf '%s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

\cmd|cmp| compares bytes: no message and status zero indicate a match.
Status one indicates different data, and a value greater than one
indicates an error such as a missing file. \cmd|"$?"| must be read
immediately after the comparison, not after another \cmd|ls|. A
filename, date or size alone doesn't show that the right bytes arrived.
If you can't bring both copies together, you can compare a digest made
by the same checksum algorithm on both endpoints (see Chapter~\ref{ch:23}).
An expected digest must come from a source you trust, not whatever was
transferred alongside a suspicious copy. An equal digest is evidence
about content, not the owner or access permissions. Consider an
independent read-back using the eventual recipient's normal access
route, with their permission, when delivery involves a restricted area.

Make the verification useful to the person waiting at the other end.
For a text agenda, opening the checked version is often enough to see
whether it has the revisions requested. A checksum can't tell you
that you chose the right draft if you faithfully transferred the wrong
one. Check the source before the transfer, check the receiving bytes
after it, and identify the version in your handoff. If the recipient
reports that the file opens but contains last month's schedule, the
next question is which copy was the source; moving the same bytes
again wouldn't help. If they can't open it, check whether they are
looking in the right location with the right account before
resending or changing access.

Finally, use the smallest transfer that meets the need. Bringing
an entire project directory down for one agenda may unnecessarily
copy older personnel notes and hidden configuration files. Don't
substitute a wildcard for a deliberate list of files when preparing
a submission. If you have several intended documents, make a private
staging directory, inspect the exact contents and transfer only that
set by the approved route. When tidying up afterwards, remove
temporary copies you no longer need under the applicable retention
rules, not the source document somebody else is still editing.

\begin{quicktable}{p{1.35in}Y}
\toprule
Check & What evidence it gives \\
\midrule
Confirm both directory contexts & Tests whether source and destination names mean what you intend before writing. \\
Preview changes & Lets you catch direction or layout mistakes; does not confirm a write. \\
Inspect destination & Shows whether a named object is present, not necessarily the intended bytes. \\
Compare content & Tests bytes or matching digests; does not confirm sharing policy or the accepted submission name. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\begin{dialoguebox}{The completed bar}
\Brian{It said 100 percent. What else could I need?}
\Stephen{At least check that it arrived in the right place.}
\Brian{I can see a file with exactly the right name.}
\Stephen{Was it there before? The label doesn't establish which version it contains.}
\Brian{But the recipient doesn't have my original copy.}
\Stephen{You can verify the received copy yourself, or compare trusted digests calculated on both sides.}
\Brian{That tells me about the bytes, not whether the recipient can open the path.}
\Stephen{Right. Check delivery and permission separately.}
\DialogueConclusion{Verify the content and the destination.}
\end{dialoguebox}

Keep both fixture directories until you have finished all exercises.
Then, before cleanup, use \cmd|pwd| to verify you are in
\cmd|"$transferlab"|. Confirm each named practice file with
\cmd|rm -i ./source/agenda.txt| and then separately with
\cmd|rm -i ./receive/agenda-received.txt|.
Then \cmd|rmdir ./source ./receive|; this refuses to remove nonempty
directories. Finally use \cmd|cd && rmdir "$transferlab"|.
If you practiced with a second copy in the exercises, remove that
explicitly before attempting the directories.

\begin{exercise}{Predict the directory layout}\label{ex:39-layout}
Keeping the same temporary directory, preview with and without the
trailing slash on the source. Check that neither run creates files.
If optional rsync is unavailable, read Figure~\ref{fig:39-slash} and
write down both predicted paths instead. Keep the lab for the next exercise.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\cmd|rsync -r -n -i ./source ./receive/| predicts
\texttt{receive/source/agenda.txt}; with \cmd|./source/| it predicts
\texttt{receive/agenda.txt}. The dry-run option prevents either
write. Any \texttt{agenda-received.txt} you already copied is a
different path and remains in place.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Prove that a similar name is not enough}\label{ex:39-content}
Write one line of different text to a confirmed unused
\texttt{./receive/agenda-revised.txt}. Compare it to the source with
\texttt{cmp}. Inspect the status and remove just this fixture.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' '18:30 close the hall' > ./receive/agenda-revised.txt
cmp ./source/agenda.txt ./receive/agenda-revised.txt
printf '%s\n' "$?"
rm -i ./receive/agenda-revised.txt
\end{terminal}
Expect a difference and status 1. If a file could not be read, resolve
that error before interpreting the check. Confirm removal only of the
new synthetic copy.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Rehearse a handoff}\label{ex:39-handoff}
Compare the original and the copy named \texttt{agenda-received.txt}.
Write a brief handoff identifying the checked filename, what the
comparison established, and one thing still unknown about delivery.
Don't send the note or the file. Clean up the original and received lab
files and remove the directories using the steps above.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\cmd|cmp ./source/agenda.txt ./receive/agenda-received.txt| should
report no differences, with status zero. A suitable note is: ``The
staged agenda-received.txt matches the source byte for byte. No remote
recipient has been given access.'' Verify the working directory and
remove only the known practice files before using \texttt{rmdir}.
\end{solution}

\begin{takeaways}
\item Say the source and the destination aloud, including which machine owns each path.
\item Check both directory contexts before putting or getting files.
\item Preview when possible, preserve the original, and verify the result before handoff.
\end{takeaways}

